Description: A2DP SBC source: send full packets only
 bluealsa encoded whatever PCM was in its buffer on each wake-up. The ALSA
 plugin feeds it through a 4096-byte pipe (1024 frames), so every full packet
 (6 SBC frames at the usual 672-byte MTU) was followed by a 2-frame one:
 measured on an RK3588 with an RTL8852BU, 38-50 % of packets full and ~104
 packets/s for 42 kB/s of audio. With a 7.5 ms BLE keyboard on the same
 controller the controller's ACL buffers were full 98 % of the time and the
 headset went short of audio. Android's SBC encoder sends a fixed number of
 frames per packet sized to the MTU (system/stack/a2dp/a2dp_sbc_encoder.cc).
 Encode only once the PCM buffer, sized to one MTU of frames, is full.
Author: rk3588-mediabox
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-10-11

--- a/src/a2dp-sbc.c
+++ b/src/a2dp-sbc.c
@@ -212,6 +212,16 @@
 
 		const int16_t *input = pcm.data;
 		size_t input_samples = ffb_len_out(&pcm);
+
+		/* Send full packets only. The PCM buffer holds exactly as many SBC
+		 * frames as fit the write MTU, so encode only once it is full. The
+		 * ALSA plugin hands PCM over through a 4096-byte pipe (1024 frames),
+		 * and encoding whatever was there sent a full packet followed by a
+		 * two-frame one: half the packets, and nearly twice the air time, for
+		 * the same audio. Android's encoder does the same (a fixed number of
+		 * frames per packet, sized to the MTU: a2dp_sbc_encoder.cc). */
+		if (input_samples < ffb_pcm_len)
+			continue;
 		size_t output_len = ffb_len_in(&bt);
 		size_t pcm_frames = 0;
 		size_t sbc_frames = 0;
